<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Olomouc</h1><p class="st-meta">1061 CE – 1200 CE (approx.) · capital Olomouc</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The main Moravian appanage duchy of the Přemyslid dynasty, seat of a bishopric and base for princes who contested the Bohemian throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretislaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Bretislaus I</a> died in 1055, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Moravia</a> was divided among his sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vratislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vratislaus</a>, then in 1061 to Otto I the Fair, whose line held it through the 12th century. The bishopric of Olomouc was restored in 1063, making the town Moravia's religious centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> princes such as Svatopluk, who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1107, and Otto III were influential figures in the dynasty's power struggles. The town grew around the castle, which included a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s palace built by Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jindrich-zdik">Jindřich Zdík</a> in the 1140s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Late in the 12th century the Moravian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> lost their separate status. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Moravia</a> became a margraviate under imperial recognition, and around 1197 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-iii-duke-of-bohemia">Vladislaus Henry</a> united it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia's</a> ecclesiastical capital; its bishopric became an archbishopric in 1777.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1061 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.82%;width:2.77%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1061 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Olomouc</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto I of Olomouc, Svatopluk of Olomouc, Otto III of Olomouc</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Czech</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Olmütz, Moravian appanage of Olomouc</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=duchy-of-olomouc" aria-label="Duchy of Olomouc on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5077 450 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Olomouc lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5397 706L5330 692M5397 706L5383 718"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5330 692h0M5383 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5397 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5397 706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Olomouc. <a href="/atlas?year=1130&amp;focus=duchy-of-olomouc">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
